std.crypto.sha256 — SHA-256 hash function
=========================================

SHA-256 is the 256-bit member of the SHA-2 family, specified in
[FIPS 180-4](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/FIPS/NIST.FIPS.180-4.pdf).
It hashes an arbitrary-length byte sequence into a 32-byte digest and
is used as a building block for HMAC, PBKDF2, and the AEAD construction
in this library. Expect collision resistance of $2^{128}$ and
preimage resistance of $2^{256}$.

The algorithm is a **Merkle–Damgård** construction over a Davies–Meyer
compression function:

$$
\mathrm{state}_{i+1} = \mathrm{state}_i \boxplus F(\mathrm{state}_i, \mathrm{block}_i)
$$

where $F$ is a 64-round ARX (Add / Rotate / Xor) mixing function and
$\boxplus$ is componentwise 32-bit wrapping addition. State is eight
32-bit words; each 512-bit (64-byte) input block updates that state,
and the final state — serialized big-endian — is the 32-byte digest.

All arithmetic is `u32` and wraps modulo $2^{32}$ by the language's
unsigned-overflow rule.

Dependencies: `std.encoding.binary` for big-endian u32 packing.

    module std.crypto.sha256

    import std.encoding.binary.{u32_be_at, put_u32_be_at}


Compression primitives
----------------------

Six mixing functions drive every round. Their purpose is to spread each
input bit over many output positions as cheaply as possible (rotations
and xors are free on most hardware; additions cost a carry).

### `rotr` — 32-bit right rotation

$$
\mathrm{rotr}(x, n) = (x \gg n) \;\lor\; (x \ll (32 - n))
$$

    rotr(x: u32, n: u32) -> u32 = (x >> n) | (x << (32u32 - n))

### `ch` — "choose"

Bit $i$ of $\mathrm{ch}(x, y, z)$ is bit $i$ of $y$ when
bit $i$ of $x$ is set, otherwise bit $i$ of $z$:

$$
\mathrm{Ch}(x, y, z) = (x \land y) \oplus (\neg x \land z)
$$

    ch(x: u32, y: u32, z: u32) -> u32 = (x & y) ^ (~x & z)

### `maj` — "majority"

Bit $i$ of $\mathrm{maj}(x, y, z)$ is set iff at least two of the
three inputs have bit $i$ set:

$$
\mathrm{Maj}(x, y, z) = (x \land y) \oplus (x \land z) \oplus (y \land z)
$$

    maj(x: u32, y: u32, z: u32) -> u32 = (x & y) ^ (x & z) ^ (y & z)

### `big_sigma0` / `big_sigma1` — state-diffusion functions ($\Sigma_0, \Sigma_1$)

Applied to the working variables `a` and `e` each round, the "big
sigmas" scatter bits across all 32 positions with three rotations:

$$
\Sigma_0(x) = \mathrm{ROTR}^{2}(x) \oplus \mathrm{ROTR}^{13}(x) \oplus \mathrm{ROTR}^{22}(x)
$$
$$
\Sigma_1(x) = \mathrm{ROTR}^{6}(x) \oplus \mathrm{ROTR}^{11}(x) \oplus \mathrm{ROTR}^{25}(x)
$$

    big_sigma0(x: u32) -> u32 = rotr(x, 2u32) ^ rotr(x, 13u32) ^ rotr(x, 22u32)

    big_sigma1(x: u32) -> u32 = rotr(x, 6u32) ^ rotr(x, 11u32) ^ rotr(x, 25u32)

### `small_sigma0` / `small_sigma1` — message-schedule functions ($\sigma_0, \sigma_1$)

The "small sigmas" drive the message-schedule expansion from 16 input
words to 64 working words. One of the three mixers is a shift (not a
rotation) so that the low bits get a distinct treatment from the
high bits:

$$
\sigma_0(x) = \mathrm{ROTR}^{7}(x) \oplus \mathrm{ROTR}^{18}(x) \oplus (x \gg 3)
$$
$$
\sigma_1(x) = \mathrm{ROTR}^{17}(x) \oplus \mathrm{ROTR}^{19}(x) \oplus (x \gg 10)
$$

    small_sigma0(x: u32) -> u32 = rotr(x, 7u32) ^ rotr(x, 18u32) ^ (x >> 3u32)

    small_sigma1(x: u32) -> u32 = rotr(x, 17u32) ^ rotr(x, 19u32) ^ (x >> 10u32)


Round constants
---------------

The `K` table holds one 32-bit constant per round — the first 32 bits
of the fractional part of $\sqrt[3]{p_i}$, where $p_i$ is the
$i$-th prime. These are "nothing-up-my-sleeve" numbers: cheap to
verify yet structureless enough that no backdoor can be hidden in them.

The initial hash state `H⁰` (set inside `sha256()` below) uses the same
trick with square roots of the first eight primes.

    val K: [64]u32 = [
        0x428a2f98u32, 0x71374491u32, 0xb5c0fbcfu32, 0xe9b5dba5u32,
        0x3956c25bu32, 0x59f111f1u32, 0x923f82a4u32, 0xab1c5ed5u32,
        0xd807aa98u32, 0x12835b01u32, 0x243185beu32, 0x550c7dc3u32,
        0x72be5d74u32, 0x80deb1feu32, 0x9bdc06a7u32, 0xc19bf174u32,
        0xe49b69c1u32, 0xefbe4786u32, 0x0fc19dc6u32, 0x240ca1ccu32,
        0x2de92c6fu32, 0x4a7484aau32, 0x5cb0a9dcu32, 0x76f988dau32,
        0x983e5152u32, 0xa831c66du32, 0xb00327c8u32, 0xbf597fc7u32,
        0xc6e00bf3u32, 0xd5a79147u32, 0x06ca6351u32, 0x14292967u32,
        0x27b70a85u32, 0x2e1b2138u32, 0x4d2c6dfcu32, 0x53380d13u32,
        0x650a7354u32, 0x766a0abbu32, 0x81c2c92eu32, 0x92722c85u32,
        0xa2bfe8a1u32, 0xa81a664bu32, 0xc24b8b70u32, 0xc76c51a3u32,
        0xd192e819u32, 0xd6990624u32, 0xf40e3585u32, 0x106aa070u32,
        0x19a4c116u32, 0x1e376c08u32, 0x2748774cu32, 0x34b0bcb5u32,
        0x391c0cb3u32, 0x4ed8aa4au32, 0x5b9cca4fu32, 0x682e6ff3u32,
        0x748f82eeu32, 0x78a5636fu32, 0x84c87814u32, 0x8cc70208u32,
        0x90befffau32, 0xa4506cebu32, 0xbef9a3f7u32, 0xc67178f2u32
    ]


Compression function
--------------------

`process_block` folds one 512-bit input block into the eight-word state.
It proceeds in three phases:

1. **Load** the 16 input words $W_0 \ldots W_{15}$ big-endian from the block.
2. **Expand** to a 64-word message schedule $W_{16} \ldots W_{63}$ via
   $$
   W_i = \sigma_1(W_{i-2}) + W_{i-7} + \sigma_0(W_{i-15}) + W_{i-16}.
   $$
3. **Run 64 rounds**, each of which mixes one schedule word and one round
   constant into eight working variables $a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h$:
   $$
   \begin{aligned}
   T_1 &= h + \Sigma_1(e) + \mathrm{Ch}(e, f, g) + K_i + W_i \\
   T_2 &= \Sigma_0(a) + \mathrm{Maj}(a, b, c) \\
   (a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h) &\leftarrow (T_1 + T_2,\; a,\; b,\; c,\; d + T_1,\; e,\; f,\; g)
   \end{aligned}
   $$

After 64 rounds the working variables are added back into the input
state — the Davies–Meyer feedforward that makes this a one-way function
even though every individual round is invertible.

    process_block(state: []u32, block: []byte, off: int)
        var w: [64]u32

Load the 16 message words big-endian.

        for i in 0..<16
            w[i] = u32_be_at(block, off + i * 4)

Expand to 64 schedule words via $\sigma_0 / \sigma_1$.

        for i in 16..<64
            w[i] = small_sigma1(w[i - 2]) + w[i - 7] + small_sigma0(w[i - 15]) + w[i - 16]

Pull the eight working variables from state.

        var a = state[0]
        var b = state[1]
        var c = state[2]
        var d = state[3]
        var e = state[4]
        var f = state[5]
        var g = state[6]
        var h = state[7]

Run 64 mixing rounds.

        for i in 0..<64
            var t1: u32 = h + big_sigma1(e)
            t1 = t1 + ch(e, f, g)
            t1 = t1 + K[i]
            t1 = t1 + w[i]
            var t2: u32 = big_sigma0(a) + maj(a, b, c)

            h = g
            g = f
            f = e
            e = d + t1
            d = c
            c = b
            b = a
            a = t1 + t2

Feed the final working variables back into the state (Davies–Meyer).

        state[0] += a
        state[1] += b
        state[2] += c
        state[3] += d
        state[4] += e
        state[5] += f
        state[6] += g
        state[7] += h


`sha256` — hash a contiguous buffer
-----------------------------------

`sha256(data, out)` hashes `data` and writes the 32-byte digest into
`out`. The output buffer must be at least 32 bytes; bytes beyond that
are not touched.

### Initial hash value `H⁰`

The eight IV words are the first 32 bits of the fractional parts of
$\sqrt{p_i}$ for the first eight primes — another nothing-up-my-sleeve
set, matching the rationale behind `K`.

### Padding

The Merkle–Damgård padding scheme appends, in order:

1. one `0x80` byte (the "1" bit followed by seven "0" bits),
2. zero or more `0x00` bytes,
3. the 64-bit big-endian length in **bits** of the original message.

Enough zero bytes are inserted so that the padded total is a multiple
of 64 bytes. When the final message remainder is 56 bytes or more, the
eight-byte length field cannot fit in the current block — the padding
spills into a second block that is zeros followed by the length.

    sha256(data: []byte, out: []byte)
        var state: [8]u32
        state[0] = 0x6a09e667u32
        state[1] = 0xbb67ae85u32
        state[2] = 0x3c6ef372u32
        state[3] = 0xa54ff53au32
        state[4] = 0x510e527fu32
        state[5] = 0x9b05688cu32
        state[6] = 0x1f83d9abu32
        state[7] = 0x5be0cd19u32

        val n = len(data)
        var i = 0

Process every complete 64-byte block.

        while i + 64 <= n
            process_block(state[:], data, i)
            i += 64

The tail is at most 63 bytes. A 128-byte local buffer is always enough
for the tail plus padding plus length — whether the padded tail fits in
one block or spills into a second.

        var block: [128]byte = ""
        val rem = n - i

        for j in 0..<rem
            block[j] = data[i + j]

        block[rem] = 0x80u8                       // "1" delimiter bit, seven zero bits
        val total_bits: i64 = i64(n) * 8i64

        if rem < 56

One-block tail: the length fits in bytes 56..63 of the same block.

            for j in 0..<8
                block[56 + j] = byte(total_bits >> i64(56 - j * 8))

            process_block(state[:], block[:], 0)
        else

Two-block tail: first block carries the "1" bit and zero padding; the
length lives in bytes 56..63 of the second block.

            for j in (rem + 1)..<64
                block[j] = 0u8

            process_block(state[:], block[:], 0)

            for j in 0..<56
                block[j] = 0u8

            for j in 0..<8
                block[56 + j] = byte(total_bits >> i64(56 - j * 8))

            process_block(state[:], block[:], 0)

Serialize the eight state words big-endian as the 32-byte digest.

        for i in 0..<8
            put_u32_be_at(out, i * 4, state[i])


`sha256_hw` — hardware-accelerated path
---------------------------------------

An ESP32-style SHA-256 MMIO accelerator lives at `SHA_BASE`. The hardware
owns the compression function — it keeps the 256-bit state internally and
computes one round of $F$ per block write — while software still handles
bit packing, padding, and block sequencing. Same call-site interface as
`sha256()`; the caller chooses which path they want.

    val SHA_BASE = 0x800160

### `process_block_hw`

Write one 512-bit block into the accelerator's `TEXT` registers (16 words
of 4 bytes each) and trigger compression. Two trigger registers exist:
`START` at offset `0x40` for the very first block (re-inits the hardware
state to `H⁰`), and `CONTINUE` at offset `0x44` for every subsequent
block (folds into the running state).

    process_block_hw(block: []byte, off: int, first: int)
        val base = SHA_BASE

        for i in 0..<16                           // pack 16 big-endian words into TEXT[0..15]
            val w = u32_be_at(block, off + i * 4)
            var p: *u32 = *u32(base + i * 4)
            *p = w

        if first != 0
            var p: *u32 = *u32(base + 0x40)       // START: reset state then compress
            *p = 1u32
        else
            var p: *u32 = *u32(base + 0x44)       // CONTINUE: compress into running state
            *p = 1u32

### `read_hw_state`

Drain the 256-bit accelerator state (eight u32 words) back into a big-
endian byte buffer.

    read_hw_state(out: []byte)
        val base = SHA_BASE
        for i in 0..<8
            var p: *u32 = *u32(base + i * 4)
            put_u32_be_at(out, i * 4, *p)

### `sha256_hw`

Drop-in replacement for `sha256()` using the accelerator. Same padding
logic; only the compression calls change. The `first` flag tracks
whether the next trigger should be `START` or `CONTINUE`.

    sha256_hw(data: []byte, out: []byte)
        val n = len(data)
        var i = 0
        var first = 1

        while i + 64 <= n
            process_block_hw(data, i, first)
            first = 0
            i += 64

        var block: [128]byte = ""
        val rem = n - i

        for j in 0..<rem
            block[j] = data[i + j]

        block[rem] = 0x80u8
        val total_bits: i64 = i64(n) * 8i64

        if rem < 56
            for j in 0..<8
                block[56 + j] = byte(total_bits >> i64(56 - j * 8))
            process_block_hw(block[:], 0, first)
        else
            for j in (rem + 1)..<64
                block[j] = 0u8
            process_block_hw(block[:], 0, first)
            first = 0
            for j in 0..<56
                block[j] = 0u8
            for j in 0..<8
                block[56 + j] = byte(total_bits >> i64(56 - j * 8))
            process_block_hw(block[:], 0, first)

        read_hw_state(out)


Tests
=====

### $\sigma_0$ consistency

Recompute $\sigma_0(x)$ from its rotation and shift parts and check
that the packaged function agrees.

    #test
    test_small_sigma0() -> unit
        val x: u32 = 0x61626380u32
        val r = small_sigma0(x)
        val r7 = rotr(x, 7u32)
        val r18 = rotr(x, 18u32)
        val s3 = x >> 3u32
        val manual = r7 ^ r18 ^ s3
        assert(r == manual, s"sigma0 consistency: fn=${i64(r)} manual=${i64(manual)}")

### Message schedule words `w[16]` and `w[17]` for `"abc"`

For the message `"abc"` the padded block has $W_0 = 0\texttt{x61626380}$,
$W_1 \ldots W_{14} = 0$, and $W_{15} = 0\texttt{x18}$ (24 bits = 3 bytes).
Then $W_{16} = \sigma_1(W_{14}) + W_9 + \sigma_0(W_1) + W_0 = W_0$,
and $W_{17} = \sigma_1(W_{15}) + W_{10} + \sigma_0(W_2) + W_1 = \sigma_1(0\texttt{x18})$.

    #test
    test_message_schedule_w16() -> unit
        val w16 = small_sigma1(0u32) + 0u32 + small_sigma0(0u32) + 0x61626380u32
        assert(w16 == 0x61626380u32, s"w16: got ${i64(w16)}")

    #test
    test_message_schedule_w17() -> unit
        val w17 = small_sigma1(0x00000018u32)
        assert(w17 == 0x000F0000u32, s"w17: got ${i64(w17)}")


### K constants integrity

Guard against bit-rot in the `K` table by cross-checking every entry
against a freshly-typed copy of the FIPS 180-4 values.

    #test
    test_k_constants() -> unit
        val expected: [64]u32 = [0x428a2f98u32, 0x71374491u32, 0xb5c0fbcfu32, 0xe9b5dba5u32, 0x3956c25bu32, 0x59f111f1u32, 0x923f82a4u32, 0xab1c5ed5u32, 0xd807aa98u32, 0x12835b01u32, 0x243185beu32, 0x550c7dc3u32, 0x72be5d74u32, 0x80deb1feu32, 0x9bdc06a7u32, 0xc19bf174u32, 0xe49b69c1u32, 0xefbe4786u32, 0x0fc19dc6u32, 0x240ca1ccu32, 0x2de92c6fu32, 0x4a7484aau32, 0x5cb0a9dcu32, 0x76f988dau32, 0x983e5152u32, 0xa831c66du32, 0xb00327c8u32, 0xbf597fc7u32, 0xc6e00bf3u32, 0xd5a79147u32, 0x06ca6351u32, 0x14292967u32, 0x27b70a85u32, 0x2e1b2138u32, 0x4d2c6dfcu32, 0x53380d13u32, 0x650a7354u32, 0x766a0abbu32, 0x81c2c92eu32, 0x92722c85u32, 0xa2bfe8a1u32, 0xa81a664bu32, 0xc24b8b70u32, 0xc76c51a3u32, 0xd192e819u32, 0xd6990624u32, 0xf40e3585u32, 0x106aa070u32, 0x19a4c116u32, 0x1e376c08u32, 0x2748774cu32, 0x34b0bcb5u32, 0x391c0cb3u32, 0x4ed8aa4au32, 0x5b9cca4fu32, 0x682e6ff3u32, 0x748f82eeu32, 0x78a5636fu32, 0x84c87814u32, 0x8cc70208u32, 0x90befffau32, 0xa4506cebu32, 0xbef9a3f7u32, 0xc67178f2u32]
        for i in 0..<64
            assert(K[i] == expected[i], s"K[$i]: got ${i64(K[i])} want ${i64(expected[i])}")


### Published FIPS test vectors

Each of the following digests is copied directly from the FIPS 180-4
Appendix A / NIST CAVS test vectors. Changing any bit of the algorithm
or constants will flip many output bits and fail one of these tests.

#### SHA-256(`""`)

Expected: `e3b0c442 98fc1c14 9afbf4c8 996fb924 27ae41e4 649b934c a495991b 7852b855`

    #test
    test_sha256_empty() -> unit
        val empty: [0]byte = ""
        var out: [32]byte
        sha256(empty[:], out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0xe3, 0xb0, 0xc4, 0x42, 0x98, 0xfc, 0x1c, 0x14, 0x9a, 0xfb, 0xf4, 0xc8, 0x99, 0x6f, 0xb9, 0x24, 0x27, 0xae, 0x41, 0xe4, 0x64, 0x9b, 0x93, 0x4c, 0xa4, 0x95, 0x99, 0x1b, 0x78, 0x52, 0xb8, 0x55]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


#### SHA-256(`"abc"`)

Expected: `ba7816bf 8f01cfea 414140de 5dae2223 b00361a3 96177a9c b410ff61 f20015ad`

    #test
    test_sha256_abc() -> unit
        val msg: [3]byte = "abc"
        var out: [32]byte
        sha256(msg[:], out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0xba, 0x78, 0x16, 0xbf, 0x8f, 0x01, 0xcf, 0xea, 0x41, 0x41, 0x40, 0xde, 0x5d, 0xae, 0x22, 0x23, 0xb0, 0x03, 0x61, 0xa3, 0x96, 0x17, 0x7a, 0x9c, 0xb4, 0x10, 0xff, 0x61, 0xf2, 0x00, 0x15, 0xad]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


#### Block loading for `"abc"`

Verifies the first and last message-schedule words of the padded "abc"
block, then runs one round of compression and cross-checks all eight
state words against the published digest — which catches bit-order
bugs in `u32_be_at` before they can corrupt every hash in the system.

    #test
    test_block_loading_abc() -> unit
        var block: [128]byte = ""
        block[0] = byte('a')
        block[1] = byte('b')
        block[2] = byte('c')
        block[3] = 0x80u8
        block[63] = 0x18u8

        val w0 = (u32(block[0]) << 24u32) | (u32(block[1]) << 16u32) | (u32(block[2]) << 8u32) | u32(block[3])
        assert(w0 == 0x61626380u32, s"w0: got ${i64(w0)}")

        val w15 = (u32(block[60]) << 24u32) | (u32(block[61]) << 16u32) | (u32(block[62]) << 8u32) | u32(block[63])
        assert(w15 == 0x00000018u32, s"w15: got ${i64(w15)}")

        var state: [8]u32
        state[0] = 0x6a09e667u32
        state[1] = 0xbb67ae85u32
        state[2] = 0x3c6ef372u32
        state[3] = 0xa54ff53au32
        state[4] = 0x510e527fu32
        state[5] = 0x9b05688cu32
        state[6] = 0x1f83d9abu32
        state[7] = 0x5be0cd19u32

        process_block(state[:], block[:], 0)

        assert(state[0] == 0xba7816bfu32, s"state[0]: ${i64(state[0])}")
        assert(state[1] == 0x8f01cfeau32, s"state[1]: ${i64(state[1])}")
        assert(state[2] == 0x414140deu32, s"state[2]: ${i64(state[2])}")
        assert(state[3] == 0x5dae2223u32, s"state[3]: ${i64(state[3])}")
        assert(state[4] == 0xb00361a3u32, s"state[4]: ${i64(state[4])}")
        assert(state[5] == 0x96177a9cu32, s"state[5]: ${i64(state[5])}")
        assert(state[6] == 0xb410ff61u32, s"state[6]: ${i64(state[6])}")
        assert(state[7] == 0xf20015adu32, s"state[7]: ${i64(state[7])}")


#### SHA-256(`"a"`)

Expected: `ca978112 ca1bbdca fac231b3 9a23dc4d a786eff8 147c4e72 b9807785 afee48bb`

    #test
    test_sha256_a() -> unit
        val msg: [1]byte = "a"
        var out: [32]byte
        sha256(msg[:], out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0xca, 0x97, 0x81, 0x12, 0xca, 0x1b, 0xbd, 0xca, 0xfa, 0xc2, 0x31, 0xb3, 0x9a, 0x23, 0xdc, 0x4d, 0xa7, 0x86, 0xef, 0xf8, 0x14, 0x7c, 0x4e, 0x72, 0xb9, 0x80, 0x77, 0x85, 0xaf, 0xee, 0x48, 0xbb]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


### Hash-function properties

Determinism (same input → same output) and sensitivity (different input
→ some output bit differs) are the two cheapest sanity checks. Neither
proves cryptographic strength, but a failure of either is fatal.

    #test
    test_sha256_deterministic() -> unit
        val msg: [5]byte = "hello"

        var out1: [32]byte
        var out2: [32]byte
        sha256(msg[:], out1[:])
        sha256(msg[:], out2[:])

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out1[i] == out2[i], "deterministic")

    #test
    test_sha256_different_inputs() -> unit
        val msg1: [1]byte = "a"
        val msg2: [1]byte = "b"

        var out1: [32]byte
        var out2: [32]byte
        sha256(msg1[:], out1[:])
        sha256(msg2[:], out2[:])

        var differs = false

        for i in 0..<32
            if out1[i] != out2[i] then differs = true

        assert(differs, "different inputs differ")


#### SHA-256(`"hello"`) — full-digest verification

Expected: `2cf24dba 5fb0a30e 26e83b2a c5b9e29e 1b161e5c 1fa7425e 73043362 938b9824`

    #test
    test_sha256_hello() -> unit
        val msg: [5]byte = "hello"
        var out: [32]byte
        sha256(msg[:], out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0x2c, 0xf2, 0x4d, 0xba, 0x5f, 0xb0, 0xa3, 0x0e, 0x26, 0xe8, 0x3b, 0x2a, 0xc5, 0xb9, 0xe2, 0x9e, 0x1b, 0x16, 0x1e, 0x5c, 0x1f, 0xa7, 0x42, 0x5e, 0x73, 0x04, 0x33, 0x62, 0x93, 0x8b, 0x98, 0x24]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


#### SHA-256 two-block message (56-byte input)

The 448-bit NIST test vector. Exactly 56 input bytes forces padding to
spill into a second block — the path where `rem >= 56` in `sha256()`.

Expected: `248d6a61 d20638b8 e5c02693 0c3e6039 a33ce459 64ff2167 f6ecedd4 19db06c1`

    #test
    test_sha256_two_block() -> unit
        val msg: [56]byte = "abcdbcdecdefdefgefghfghighijhijkijkljklmklmnlmnomnopnopq"
        var out: [32]byte
        sha256(msg[:], out[:])

        val expected: [32]byte = [0x24, 0x8d, 0x6a, 0x61, 0xd2, 0x06, 0x38, 0xb8, 0xe5, 0xc0, 0x26, 0x93, 0x0c, 0x3e, 0x60, 0x39, 0xa3, 0x3c, 0xe4, 0x59, 0x64, 0xff, 0x21, 0x67, 0xf6, 0xec, 0xed, 0xd4, 0x19, 0xdb, 0x06, 0xc1]

        for i in 0..<32
            assert(out[i] == expected[i], s"byte $i: got ${int(out[i])} want ${int(expected[i])}")


Design notes
------------

**Big-endian word loading.** SHA-256 operates on 32-bit big-endian
words, unlike ChaCha20 which uses little-endian. `process_block`
converts bytes to `u32` words at the input boundary; the serializer
does the reverse at the output boundary.

**Two-block final padding.** When the message remainder is 56 bytes or
more, the 9-byte trailer (`0x80` delimiter + eight-byte length) does
not fit in the current block. Rather than branch deeper in the
compression loop, `sha256()` allocates a single 128-byte local buffer
that is always large enough for "worst-case tail + padding + length"
and selects between one-block and two-block padding there.

**No streaming API.** The current interface hashes a contiguous byte
buffer in one call. A streaming API (`sha256_init` / `sha256_update` /
`sha256_finalize`) would allow incremental hashing across a sequence
of read calls, but is deferred until there is a concrete use case
(e.g. hashing data flowing from a SLIX file handle).
